### Steps:

1. Open your code editor and navigate to `src/features/tasks/components/task-form-modal.tsx`.
2. Locate the `<FormBody>` component. It should contain the form content.
3. Update the padding classes within the `<FormBody>` component to `p-4 sm:p-6`. This will adjust the padding for small screens (`sm` stands for small media query).
4. Ensure that the modal action buttons remain visible and not covered by the increased padding. You can adjust their position if needed.

### Acceptance Criteria:

1. **Modal padding adjusts cleanly with `p-4 sm:p-6`**: After applying the updated padding classes, the padding should change smoothly for both desktop and mobile views.
2. **All form fields and action buttons remain fully functional**: Ensure that none of the form fields or action buttons are affected by the padding change, and they continue to work as expected.

Here's an example of the updated `<FormBody>` component:

```tsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { Form, Input, Button } from '@ui5/webcomponents-react';

const TaskFormModal: React.FC<TaskFormModalProps> = ({ onSubmit, onCancel }) => {
  const [task, setTask] = useState({
    title: '',
    description: '',
  });

  const handleSubmit = () => {
    onSubmit(task);
    onCancel();
  };

  return (
    <Form>
      <Form.Item label="Title">
        <Input value={task.title} onChange={(e) => setTask({ ...task, title: e.target.value })} />
      </Form.Item>
      <Form.Item label="Description">
        <Input value={task.description} onChange={(e) => setTask({ ...task, description: e.target.value })} />
      </Form.Item>
      <Button type="primary" onClick={handleSubmit}>
        Save Task
      </Button>
      <Button onClick={onCancel}>Cancel</Button>
    </Form>
  );
};

export default TaskFormModal;
```


